{
  "_about": "`attitude`: \"aggressive\" enemies attack anyone who comes within `detection`; \"passive\" ones (yellow names, like the wild boar) mind their own business until struck. An enemy with `dummy: true` (the training dummy) never moves, attacks or dies: its health fills again when it reaches zero or after a few seconds out of combat. Enemy types: stats, behaviour and what they drop. Where they stand is in world.json (enemySpawns). `type` (fodder, pack_beast, normal, skirmisher, armoured, elite, heavy, boss) and `level` work out health, damage, attackInterval, armour (or `armourClass`: none, light, armoured, heavy), an elite's special and an aimed shot (src/data/scaling.js); a number stated here wins. `runSpeed`: chasing, how fast it runs as a percent of the player's base move speed (CONFIG.chase in game/core/db.gd gives each type a default, 110-120 for most; fast beasts up to 150, shamblers under 100). `respawn`: seconds after a kill before that spawn point brings the enemy back (0 = never, for bosses and story enemies). `skin`: the loot table a beast's body yields when skinned (with Skinning and a knife); the rank needed comes from its level (src/systems/loot.js skinRank). `base`: a level variant (WoW's one creature entry per zone) names the enemy it copies and states only what differs. `maxLevel`: each spawn rolls its level from `level` to `maxLevel`. `worldDrops: false`: it drops nothing from the level's world-drop band (beasts). An `imported` block swaps in an imported, textured model and the fields that go with it (e.g. { \"model\": \"rat\", \"scale\": 1.5 }) while CONFIG.models.imported is on (game/core/db.gd); the entry's own fields are the code-built version to switch back to.",
